 potential to grow taller and wider, and often to live longer, than herbaceous plants.</span></span> only at base  ..... <a href="/eflora/keys/IJM_key_Group8.php" class="key-link">Group 8</a></div></div>  <div class="key-row indent-28"> <span class="key-number"><a href="#11" id="11_prime">11&prime;</a></span><div class="key-content"><span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">Tree<span class="term-popup">A woody plant of medium to tall maximum height, with generally one trunk from the base.  (e.g., Carnegiea gigantea)</span></span> or&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">shrub<span class="term-popup">A woody plant of relatively short maximum height,  with generally many branches from the base.</span></span>, conspicuously&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">woody<span class="term-popup">A woody plant is a perennial that produces extensive wood and bark. This hard, supportive tissue allows these plants (ex. trees, shrubs, some vines) to persist year-round. Wood, which consists mainly of xylem (reinforced conducting) tissue, transports water and provides support so that these plants have potential to grow taller and wider, and often to live longer, than herbaceous plants.</span></span>  ..... <a href="/eflora/keys/IJM_key_Group9.php" class="key-link">Group 9</a></div></div>  <div class="key-row indent-14"> <span class="key-number"><a href="#8" id="8_prime">8&prime;</a></span><div class="key-content">Specimens with&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">bisexual<span class="term-popup"><img src="/eflora/common/images/morph/bisexual-flower.jpg" class="term-img" alt="Morphology: bisexual">Both male and female reproductive parts occurring and functional in the same plant or structure (e.g., flower, spikelet, inflorescence).</span></span> flowers or with both&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">staminate<span class="term-popup">Pertaining to flowers, inflorescences, or plants with fertile stamens but sterile or missing pistils.  (e.g., Salix laevigata flower)</span></span> and&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">pistillate<span class="term-popup">Pertaining to flowers, inflorescences, or plants with fertile pistils but sterile or missing stamens.  (e.g., Salix laevigata flower)</span></span> flowers [on same or different individuals]; plants&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">bisexual<span class="term-popup"><img src="/eflora/common/images/morph/bisexual-flower.jpg" class="term-img" alt="Morphology: bisexual">Both male and female reproductive parts occurring and functional in the same plant or structure (e.g., flower, spikelet, inflorescence).</span></span>,&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">dioecious<span class="term-popup">Pertaining to a taxon in which individuals produce either male or female reproductive structures, and do not produce bisexual reproductive structures.  (e.g., Salix laevigata)</span></span>, or&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">monoecious<span class="term-popup">Pertaining to a taxon in which individuals produce both male and female reproductive structures, and do not produce bisexual reproductive structures.  (e.g., Alnus rhombifolia)</span></span> (occasionally with mixture of&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">bisexual<span class="term-popup"><img src="/eflora/common/images/morph/bisexual-flower.jpg" class="term-img" alt="Morphology: bisexual">Both male and female reproductive parts occurring and functional in the same plant or structure (e.g., flower, spikelet, inflorescence).</span></span> and&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">unisexual<span class="term-popup">Either male or female reproductive parts occurring and functional in the same plant or structure (e.g., flower, spikelet, inforescence).</span></span> flowers)</div></div>  <div class="key-row indent-21"> <span class="key-number"><a href="#12_prime" id="12">12.</a></span><div class="key-content"><span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">Pistils<span class="term-popup">Female reproductive structure of a flower, composed of an ovule-containing ovary at the base, one or more pollen-receiving stigmas at the tip, and generally one or more styles between ovary and stigma.  A flower may have one or more simple pistils (each a single, free carpel with a single ovary chamber, placenta, and stigma) or one compound pistil (two or more fused or partially fused carpels, the exact number often equaling the number of ovary lobes, ovary chambers, placentas, styles, or stigmas).</span></span> 2 or more per flower [carpels > 1,&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">free<span class="term-popup">Neither fused to nor adherent to other parts; distinct, separate.</span></span> to the base]  ..... <a href="/eflora/keys/IJM_key_Group10.php" class="key-link">Group 10</a></div></div>  <div class="key-row indent-21"> <span class="key-number"><a href="#12" id="12_prime">12&prime;</a></span><div class="key-content"><span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">Pistil<span class="term-popup">Female reproductive structure of a flower, composed of an ovule-containing ovary at the base, one or more pollen-receiving stigmas at the tip, and generally one or more styles between ovary and stigma.  A flower may have one or more simple pistils (each a single, free carpel with a single ovary chamber, placenta, and stigma) or one compound pistil (two or more fused or partially fused carpels, the exact number often equaling the number of ovary lobes, ovary chambers, placentas, styles, or stigmas).</span></span> 1 per flower [carpel 1 or&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">carpels<span class="term-popup">The basic female structure of a flower, evolved from a fertile leaf.  Carpels are free or variously fused into a compound pistil, the number of carpels then often equal to the number of stigmas, styles, or chambers of the ovary.</span></span> > 1&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">fused<span class="term-popup">United, as the petals together into a corolla tube or stamens onto petals; neither free nor adherent.</span></span> at least proximally]</div></div>  <div class="key-row indent-28"> <span class="key-number"><a href="#13_prime" id="13">13.</a></span><div class="key-content"><span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">Perianth<span class="term-popup">Calyx and corolla collectively, whether or not they are distinguishable.</span></span> 0 or in a single&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">whorl<span class="term-popup">Arranged in groups of three or more at nodes or positions along an axis (e.g., three leaves per node).</span></span> [appearing to be either&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">sepals<span class="term-popup">Individual member of the calyx, whether fused or not; if fused, often equal in number to the number of calyx lobes; generally green.</span></span> or&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">petals<span class="term-popup">Individual member of the corolla, whether fused or not; if fused, often equal in number to the number of corolla lobes; often conspicuously colored.</span></span> but not both], sometimes&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">reduced<span class="term-popup">Smaller, less lobed, simpler, etc.</span></span> to scale-like or bristle-like structures</div></div>  <div class="key-row indent-35"> <span class="key-number"><a href="#14_prime" id="14">14.</a></span><div class="key-content"><span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">Tree<span class="term-popup">A woody plant of medium to tall maximum height, with generally one trunk from the base.  (e.g., Carnegiea gigantea)</span></span> or&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">shrub<span class="term-popup">A woody plant of relatively short maximum height,  with generally many branches from the base.</span></span>, conspicuously&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">woody<span class="term-popup">A woody plant is a perennial that produces extensive wood and bark. This hard, supportive tissue allows these plants (ex. trees, shrubs, some vines) to persist year-round. Wood, which consists mainly of xylem (reinforced conducting) tissue, transports water and provides support so that these plants have potential to grow taller and wider, and often to live longer, than herbaceous plants.</span></span></div></div>  <div class="key-row indent-42"> <span class="key-number"><a href="#15_prime" id="15">15.</a></span><div class="key-content"><span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">Inflorescence<span class="term-popup">An entire cluster or aggregation of flowers and associated structures (e.g., axes, bracts, bractlets, pedicels); often difficult to determine as to type and boundaries but generally excluding full-sized foliage leaves.</span></span>, at least the&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">staminate<span class="term-popup">Pertaining to flowers, inflorescences, or plants with fertile stamens but sterile or missing pistils.  (e.g., Salix laevigata flower)</span></span>, a&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">catkin<span class="term-popup"><img src="/eflora/common/images/morph/catkin.jpg" class="term-img" alt="Morphology: catkin">Spike or spike-like (e.g., Alnus) inflorescence of unisexual flowers with inconspicuous perianths (generally wind-pollinated), usually pendent and often with conspicuous bracts.</span></span> or catkin-like&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">spike<span class="term-popup"><img src="/eflora/common/images/morph/spike.jpg" class="term-img" alt="Morphology: spike">1. In flowering plants excluding Asteraceae, Cyperaceae, Poaceae, and some other groups, an unbranched inflorescence in which the flowers are sessile and nearly always open from the bottom to the top of the inflorescence. 2. In Asteraceae, Cyperaceae,  Poaceae, and some other groups, a spike-like inflorescence is one in which the inflorescence units (e.g., heads in Asteraceae; spikelets in Cyperaceae and Poaceae), instead of individual flowers, are sessile and attached directly to the main axis of the inflorescence, not to branches, and in which floral development may or may not proceed as in 1.</span></span>; flowers&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">unisexual<span class="term-popup">Either male or female reproductive parts occurring and functional in the same plant or structure (e.g., flower, spikelet, inforescence).</span></span>  ..... <a href="/eflora/keys/IJM_key_Group11.php" class="key-link">Group 11</a></div></div>  <div class="key-row indent-42"> <span class="key-number"><a href="#15" id="15_prime">15&prime;</a></span><div class="key-content"><span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">Inflorescence<span class="term-popup">An entire cluster or aggregation of flowers and associated structures (e.g., axes, bracts, bractlets, pedicels); often difficult to determine as to type and boundaries but generally excluding full-sized foliage leaves.</span></span> various but not catkin-like; flowers&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">bisexual<span class="term-popup"><img src="/eflora/common/images/morph/bisexual-flower.jpg" class="term-img" alt="Morphology: bisexual">Both male and female reproductive parts occurring and functional in the same plant or structure (e.g., flower, spikelet, inflorescence).</span></span> or&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">unisexual<span class="term-popup">Either male or female reproductive parts occurring and functional in the same plant or structure (e.g., flower, spikelet, inforescence).</span></span>  ..... <a href="/eflora/keys/IJM_key_Group12.php" class="key-link">Group 12</a></div></div>  <div class="key-row indent-35"> <span class="key-number"><a href="#14" id="14_prime">14&prime;</a></span><div class="key-content"><span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">Herb<span class="term-popup">Plant that, at least above ground, is generally non-woody and of less than one year or growing season in duration.</span></span> or&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">subshrub<span class="term-popup">A plant with the lower stems woody, the upper stems and twigs not woody (or less so) and dying back seasonally.</span></span>,&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">woody<span class="term-popup">A woody plant is a perennial that produces extensive wood and bark. This hard, supportive tissue allows these plants (ex. trees, shrubs, some vines) to persist year-round. Wood, which consists mainly of xylem (reinforced conducting) tissue, transports water and provides support so that these plants have potential to grow taller and wider, and often to live longer, than herbaceous plants.</span></span>&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">proximally<span class="term-popup">Closer to the base, origin, or point of attachment, or farther away from the edge or tip.</span></span> if at all</div></div>  <div class="key-row indent-42"> <span class="key-number"><a href="#16_prime" id="16">16.</a></span><div class="key-content"><span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">Leaf<span class="term-popup">Organ arising from a stem, generally composed of a stalk (petiole) and a flat, expanded, green, photosynthetic area (blade); distinguished from a leaflet by the presence in its axil of a bud, branch, thorn, or flower; sometimes with lateral, basal appendages (stipules); either simple (toothed, lobed, or dissected but not divided into leaflets) or compound (divided into leaflets).</span></span> venation parallel or&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">leaves<span class="term-popup">Organ arising from a stem, generally composed of a stalk (petiole) and a flat, expanded, green, photosynthetic area (blade); distinguished from a leaflet by the presence in its axil of a bud, branch, thorn, or flower; sometimes with lateral, basal appendages (stipules); either simple (toothed, lobed, or dissected but not divided into leaflets) or compound (divided into leaflets).</span></span> and&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">stem<span class="term-popup">Generally above-ground  but sometimes below-ground axis or axes of a plant; distinguished from roots by bearing leaves, leaf scars, axillary buds, flowers, nodes, etc.</span></span> not differentiated  ..... <a href="/eflora/keys/IJM_key_Group13.php" class="key-link">Group 13</a></div></div>  <div class="key-row indent-42"> <span class="key-number"><a href="#16" id="16_prime">16&prime;</a></span><div class="key-content"><span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">Leaf<span class="term-popup">Organ arising from a stem, generally composed of a stalk (petiole) and a flat, expanded, green, photosynthetic area (blade); distinguished from a leaflet by the presence in its axil of a bud, branch, thorn, or flower; sometimes with lateral, basal appendages (stipules); either simple (toothed, lobed, or dissected but not divided into leaflets) or compound (divided into leaflets).</span></span> venation generally&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">pinnate<span class="term-popup"><img src="/eflora/common/images/morph/odd-pinnate.jpg" class="term-img" alt="Morphology: pinnate">Feather-like; pertaining to veins, lobes, leaflets, or other structures arranged in two dimensions (i.e., in one plane) along either side of an axis; a leaf is odd-pinnate if there is a terminal leaflet, even-pinnate if there is not, and either odd- or even-pinnate may be 1-pinnate (blade divided into primary leaflets), 2-pinnate (primary leaflets divided into secondary leaflets), 3-pinnate (secondary leaflets divided into tertiary leaflets), etc.</span></span> or&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">palmate<span class="term-popup"><img src="/eflora/common/images/morph/palmate.jpg" class="term-img" alt="Morphology: palmate">More than two structures or parts (e.g., veins, lobes, or leaflets) radiating from a common point in two dimensions (i.e., in one plane).</span></span> (sometimes only midvein evident) or&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">leaves<span class="term-popup">Organ arising from a stem, generally composed of a stalk (petiole) and a flat, expanded, green, photosynthetic area (blade); distinguished from a leaflet by the presence in its axil of a bud, branch, thorn, or flower; sometimes with lateral, basal appendages (stipules); either simple (toothed, lobed, or dissected but not divided into leaflets) or compound (divided into leaflets).</span></span>&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">reduced<span class="term-popup">Smaller, less lobed, simpler, etc.</span></span> to bladeless&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">scales<span class="term-popup">1. Wide, appressed, membranous, epidermal outgrowth (e.g., Cheilanthes covillei) 2. Structure partially or entirely covering an over-wintering bud (bud scale) (e.g., Salix goodingii bud).  3. In gymnosperms, a woody, seed-bearing structure attached to the cone axis (cone scale) (e.g., Abies magnifica).  4. In Asteraceae, a flat, membranous pappus element (e.g., Hymenoxys hoopesii).  Leaves or bracts may be scale-like in one or more of the preceding ways.</span></span></div></div>  <div class="key-row indent-49"> <span class="key-number"><a href="#17_prime" id="17">17.</a></span><div class="key-content"><span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">Ovary<span class="term-popup">Ovule-bearing, usually wider, basal portion of pistil, normally developing into a fruit as ovules become seeds; may be simple (one carpel, one chamber) or compound (two or more carpels, one or more chambers).</span></span>&nbsp;<span class="term-trigger" tabindex="0">inferior<span class="term-popup">An ovary that is fused to the fused lower portions of the perianth and stamens (i.e., to the hypanthium), to the extent that these structures appear to arise at or above its summit</span></span>  ..... <a href="/eflora/keys/IJM_key_Group14.php" class="key-link">Group 14</a></div></div>  <div class="key-row indent-49"> <span class="key-number"><a href="#17" id="17_prime">17&prime;</a></span><div class="key-conten